-- A venue's position on the globe, and the two constraints that keep it a
-- position.
--
-- Generated by `pnpm --filter @vyloop/db db:generate` and reviewed by hand, like
-- `0011_material_odin.sql`, `0012_outstanding_flatman.sql` and
-- `0013_sour_apocalypse.sql`.
--
-- ## What this table gains
--
-- `branches` could name a venue and give its address. It could not say where
-- that venue *is*, which is what makes "the branch near you" and "a message
-- aimed at this location" expressible at all.
--
-- Five columns, all nullable:
--
--   - `latitude` and `longitude`, in decimal degrees.
--   - `geohash`, a server-computed cache of the pair, so that "venues inside
--     this cell" is a prefix match on a string rather than a scan with a
--     distance function.
--   - `geohash_precision`, because the cell a geohash denotes depends on how
--     many characters it has, and the string alone does not say how wide its
--     cell is.
--   - `located_at`, when the venue asserted this position.
--
-- ## Why `located_at` and not `updated_at`
--
-- `branches` has no `updated_at` column, and adding one would describe a
-- lifecycle the table does not have: a branch row is created and occasionally
-- corrected, it is not a row that is edited continuously. `located_at` says
-- exactly one thing — the position was set at this moment — and a reader who
-- wants to know when the venue was last renamed is asking a question this
-- table does not currently answer.
--
-- ## Why nullable, and why that is not a default
--
-- `NULL` means "this venue has not registered a position". That is a state
-- worth having: it is distinct from "the position is 0,0", and the query that
-- looks for nearby venues has to be able to exclude a venue that has not
-- said where it is. A default of `0,0` would put every existing branch in the
-- Gulf of Guinea and make that exclusion impossible.
--
-- ## Why the geohash is derived, never authoritative
--
-- The decimal degrees are the truth; the geohash is a cache of them. The
-- server recomputes it on every write, so a stale cache cannot outlive the
-- change that caused it. That is why there is no trigger keeping the two in
-- step: the trigger would be a second place that has to be right, and the
-- write path is already the one place that cannot be skipped.
--
-- ## The two constraints
--
-- A position with only one coordinate is not a position, and a position
-- outside the globe is not a position. Both are enforced by the database
-- rather than by the route that happens to write it today, because a check
-- constraint is the only thing that survives a second writer, a script, and a
-- future endpoint that forgets to validate its input.
--
-- The range constraint is written so that a row with no coordinates passes it:
-- a check is satisfied unless it evaluates to false, and a null comparison is
-- unknown. That leaves the pair constraint to own the "both or neither"
-- question, and it keeps the two from having to agree about what null means.
--
-- ## Why the index is here
--
-- The nearby-venues lookup is a prefix match on the geohash, which is a range
-- scan over the index. Without it, "venues around this point" is a scan of
-- every branch in the system — which grows with the platform rather than with
-- the question being asked of it.
--
-- ## What this migration deliberately does NOT do
--
-- **It does not enable row-level security.** `branches` is listed in
-- `BUSINESS_ID_TABLES`, so it receives a policy when the separate, reviewed
-- RLS rollout runs. That rollout is this table's isolation story and it must
-- not arrive by accident in a column addition.
--
-- **It does not backfill.** A position that was never registered cannot be
-- recovered from the street address that is already there, and a guess would
-- be worse than an absence: a wrong position would put a venue's customers in
-- the wrong city.
--
-- **It does not migrate any customer location.** No customer device position
-- is stored anywhere in this system, and this migration does not start.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
ALTER TABLE "branches" ADD COLUMN "latitude" numeric(9, 6);--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "branches" ADD COLUMN "longitude" numeric(9, 6);--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "branches" ADD COLUMN "geohash" text;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "branches" ADD COLUMN "geohash_precision" smallint;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "branches" ADD COLUMN "located_at" timestamp with time zone;--> statement-breakpoint
CREATE INDEX "idx_branches_geohash" ON "branches" USING btree ("geohash");--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "branches" ADD CONSTRAINT "branches_coordinates_pair_chk" CHECK (("branches"."latitude" is null) = ("branches"."longitude" is null));--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "branches" ADD CONSTRAINT "branches_coordinates_range_chk" CHECK ("branches"."latitude" between -90 and 90 and "branches"."longitude" between -180 and 180);
